---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Interlude from '../../components/Interlude.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Moon Phases', href: '/explore/moon-phases/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Moon Phases — working with the lunar cycle"
  description="The 29.5-day lunar cycle explained phase by phase: new moon, first quarter, full moon, last quarter, and how to use the rhythm without turning it into a rulebook."
  path="/explore/moon-phases/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Working with the moon phases</h1>
        <p class="lede">The lunar cycle is astrology's most accessible rhythm — visible from your window, roughly 29.5 days long, and a workable structure for starting, reviewing, and finishing things.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.src}
        srcset={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.alt}
        width={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.width}
        height={ocean.seaStackMoonrise.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The cycle itself</h2>
      <p>
        A complete cycle from one new moon to the next — a <em>lunation</em> — takes about 29.5
        days. It's driven by the changing angle between the Moon and the Sun as seen from Earth,
        which is why the Moon appears to grow and shrink on a schedule you can set a calendar by.
      </p>
      <p>
        Astrologers divide it into eight phases, but four carry most of the practical weight.
      </p>

      <h2>The four turning points</h2>
      <h3>New moon — the seed</h3>
      <p>
        The Moon sits alongside the Sun and is invisible from Earth. Traditionally the start of the
        cycle: a natural point for setting intentions and beginning things quietly, before there's
        anything to show. The sign it falls in colors the whole month, which is why lunation letters
        are written per new moon.
      </p>
      <h3>First quarter — the friction</h3>
      <p>
        About seven days later the Moon forms a <a href="/explore/aspects/square/">square</a> to the
        Sun. Reliably the point where a new intention meets its first real obstacle. Useful question:
        what adjustment does this actually need — and is the resistance information or just
        discomfort?
      </p>
      <h3>Full moon — the illumination</h3>
      <p>
        About fourteen days in, the Moon opposes the Sun and is fully lit. Things come to a head:
        results become visible, feelings run high, and what was hidden tends to surface. Full moons
        are better for seeing clearly and completing than for launching.
      </p>
      <h3>Last quarter — the release</h3>
      <p>
        Around day twenty-two, the second square. The natural point for pruning: finishing what's
        worth finishing, letting go of what isn't, and clearing space before the next new moon.
      </p>

      <h2>Using it without over-using it</h2>
      <p>
        The lunar cycle is a useful container precisely because it's short. A month is long enough
        to attempt something and short enough that reviewing it four times isn't a burden. Set an
        intention at the new moon, check the friction at the first quarter, look honestly at results
        at the full moon, and clear the decks at the last quarter — that's the whole practice, and
        it works whether or not you believe anything about the Moon.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          A word of caution: the moon cycle has become a marketplace for elaborate rituals and rigid
          rules about what you must never do on which day. None of that is required, and the anxiety
          it produces defeats the purpose. If a practice makes you calmer and more deliberate, it's
          working. If it makes you nervous about a full moon, it isn't.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <Interlude
    image={ocean.currents}
    eyebrow="One month at a time"
    heading="If a practice makes you calmer and more deliberate, it's working."
    body="Set an intention at the new moon, check the friction at the first quarter, look honestly at results at the full, clear the decks at the last. That is the whole practice."
  />


  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Making it personal</h2>
      <p>
        The general cycle affects everyone loosely. It becomes specific when you know which
        <a href="/explore/houses/">house</a> a given lunation falls in for your chart — that's the
        area of life the month is asking about. Your natal Moon's own phase at birth, and the sign
        it occupied, add another layer entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        See the <a href="/current-sky/">current new and full moons</a> with dates and signs, or read
        about <a href="/explore/planets/moon/">the Moon in the chart</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Which houses are your lunations lighting up?"
    body="A monthly one-on-one reads the month's movements against your chart specifically — not the general forecast."
    buttonLabel="Monthly Personal Transits"
    buttonHref="/readings/monthly-transits/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
